.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt CALDAV 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm caldav
.Nd list, show, add and change events and tasks on a CalDAV server
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl AjnT
.Op Fl B Ns Op Ar dir
.Op Fl c Ar calendar
.Op Fl p Ar password
.Op Fl U Ar url
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Op Fl -debug
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Op Fl a Ar line
.Op Fl D Ar text
.Op Fl d Ar prop
.Op Fl f Ar when
.Op Fl l Ar text
.Op Fl N Ar count
.Op Fl S Ar text
.Op Fl s Ar line
.Op Fl t Ar when
.Op Fl -alarm Ar length
.Op Fl -color Ar rrggbb
.Op Fl -due Ar when
.Op Fl -duration Ar length
.Op Fl -end Ar when
.Op Fl -name Ar text
.Op Fl -start Ar when
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads and changes one calendar on a CalDAV server: its events and
tasks, and its name, colour and description.
Options and the command may come in any order.
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm calendars
The calendars of the calendar home, one line each: id, name, and the
components it holds
.Pq Ql VEVENT,VTODO .
.It Cm list Op Ar regex
The events from
.Fl f
to
.Fl t ,
today and the 30 days after by default, recurrences expanded by the
server into their instances, in order, one line each: when, summary
and UID; with
.Fl T ,
the tasks instead, all of them unless
.Fl f
or
.Fl t
is given, with their due date and status.
A
.Ar regex
keeps those whose summary, location, description or categories it
matches, without regard to case.
.It Cm show Ar event
The event's iCalendar object, as the server holds it.
.It Cm add
A new event made from the options: it needs a summary
.Pq Fl S
and a start
.Pq Fl -start ;
without an end or a duration it lasts an hour, or a day for an
all-day start.
With
.Fl T ,
a task, which needs only its summary.
.It Cm set Ar event
The event changed by the options.
.It Cm edit Ar event
The event opened in
.Ev EDITOR ,
and stored when the file was changed.
A file that does not parse is offered back to the editor, when there is
a terminal to ask on.
.It Cm import Ar file ...
The events of iCalendar files, or of stdin for
.Ql - ,
stored one object per UID with the time zones they name: an event whose
UID the calendar has replaces it, any other is created.
When several files hold the same UID, the last one wins, so a
.Fl B
directory restores to its newest copies.
An object the server refuses is reported and the others still go.
.It Cm delete Ar event
The event deleted, the whole series of a recurring one.
.It Cm backup Op Ar file
Every object of the calendar saved as one iCalendar file, each time
zone once: to
.Ar file ,
to stdout for
.Ql - ,
or by default to a new
.Pa CALENDAR-TIMESTAMP.ics
in the current directory, named after
.Fl c .
.Cm import
restores it.
.It Cm props
The calendar's name, colour and description; with
.Fl -name ,
.Fl -color
or
.Fl D ,
those are changed first, an empty value removing one.
.El
.Pp
An
.Ar event
is a UID, the name of its resource without
.Pa .ics ,
or a regular expression matching the summary of exactly one event, of
those between
.Fl f
and
.Fl t
when either is given; a regex matching several lists them and fails.
.Pp
A
.Ar when
is a date
.Pq Ql 2026-10-15 ,
a whole day; a date and a time
.Pq Ql "2026-10-15 14:00" ;
a time alone, today or on the day of the start
.Pq Ql 14:00 ;
or a day counted from today, with or without a time:
.Ql today ,
.Ql "tomorrow 9:30" ,
.Ql "+3 10:00" .
A date as the end is the last day, inclusive.
A
.Ar length
is a number of minutes
.Pq Ql 90 ,
or weeks, days, hours and minutes
.Pq Ql 1h30m , Ql 2d , Ql 1w .
Times are written in the local time zone, named by reference
.Pq Ql DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Paris ,
the zone of
.Ev TZ
or of
.Pa /etc/localtime ;
whole days as dates.
.Pp
Every change is conditional, so a newer version is never overwritten:
an event is created only where none is
.Pq Ql If-None-Match: * ,
and replaced or deleted only in the version read
.Pq Ql If-Match .
When it changed on the server meanwhile, the change fails;
.Cm edit
then keeps the edited copy and names it.
A change stamps the event's
.Ql DTSTAMP
and
.Ql LAST-MODIFIED ,
and a new time also raises its
.Ql SEQUENCE ,
so that attendees' copies follow.
.Ss The server and the calendar
With
.Fl U ,
the URL is the calendar, and its parent the calendar home.
Without it, the home is found from the domain of the user: the
.Ql _caldavs._tcp
SRV record of the domain names the server, its TXT record
.Ql path=
the path, and the home is
.Pa https://server:port/path/user/USER/ ;
.Fl c
then picks a calendar in it, by id or by name.
.Ss Credentials
The user is
.Fl u ,
else
.Ev CALDAV_USER ,
else, with
.Fl U ,
the login of the entry for the URL's host in
.Pa ~/.authinfo ,
then
.Pa ~/.netrc ;
else
.Ev EMAIL .
Without
.Fl U
the user must be one of the options or variables, since the server is
found from it.
The password is
.Fl p ,
else
.Ev CALDAV_PASSWORD ,
else the password of the server's entry, that of the URL's host or of
the server found, when its login is the user's, else asked on the
terminal.
In those files, lines starting with
.Ql #
are skipped, and a trailing backslash joins a line to the next.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl A , Fl -all
.Cm list :
every calendar holding the component listed, each line starting with
the calendar's name.
.It Fl a Ar line , Fl -add Ns = Ns Ar line
Add an iCalendar content line, such as
.Ql CATEGORIES:Work ,
to the event; repeatable.
.It Fl -alarm Ns = Ns Ar length
Replace the event's reminders by one, this long before it;
.Ql none
removes them all.
.It Fl B Ns Op Ar dir , Fl -backup Ns Op = Ns Ar dir
Save the server's version of each event before changing or deleting
it, as
.Pa UID-TIMESTAMP.ics
in
.Ar dir ,
by default
.Pa ~/.local/share/caldav .
The directory is attached:
.Fl B Ns Pa dir .
.It Fl c Ar calendar , Fl -calendar Ns = Ns Ar calendar
The calendar, by id or name;
.Ql Default
by default.
.It Fl -color Ns = Ns Ar rrggbb
.Cm props :
set the colour, in hex, with or without its
.Ql # ,
and with an alpha byte if wanted.
.It Fl D Ar text , Fl -description Ns = Ns Ar text
Set the description, of the event or, for
.Cm props ,
of the calendar.
.It Fl d Ar prop , Fl -delete Ns = Ns Ar prop
Remove the content lines named
.Ar prop ,
in the form
.Ar name Ns Op ; Ns Ar param Ns Op : Ns Ar value :
.Ql CATEGORIES
removes them all,
.Ql ATTENDEE:mailto:jo@example.org
that one; repeatable.
One matching nothing is warned about.
.It Fl -debug
Print the HTTP exchange on stderr.
.It Fl -due Ns = Ns Ar when
Set a task's due time.
.It Fl -duration Ns = Ns Ar length
Set the end, this long after the start.
.It Fl -end Ns = Ns Ar when
Set the end, which must be of the start's kind, a date or a time, and
after it.
.It Fl f Ar when , Fl -from Ns = Ns Ar when
The start of the window
.Cm list
shows, and of the events an
.Ar event
is looked for among.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl j , Fl -json
Print JSON: one document for
.Cm calendars ,
.Cm list ,
.Cm show
and
.Cm props ,
one object a line for each change.
.It Fl l Ar text , Fl -location Ns = Ns Ar text
Set the location.
.It Fl N Ar count , Fl -limit Ns = Ns Ar count
.Cm list :
the first
.Ar count
lines only.
.It Fl n , Fl -dry-run
Change nothing: print the object that would be stored
.Pq with Fl j , a JSON object holding it ,
the deletion that would be made, or the request
.Cm props
would send.
.It Fl -name Ns = Ns Ar text
.Cm props :
set the calendar's name.
.It Fl p Ar password , Fl -password Ns = Ns Ar password
The password
.Pq Sx Credentials .
.It Fl S Ar text , Fl -summary Ns = Ns Ar text
Set the summary, the event's title.
.It Fl s Ar line , Fl -set Ns = Ns Ar line
Replace every content line of that name by this one; repeatable.
.Fl d ,
then
.Fl s ,
then
.Fl a
are applied, to the event itself and not to its modified occurrences.
.It Fl -start Ns = Ns Ar when
Set the start.
An event moved by its start alone keeps its length.
A recurring event with modified occurrences is not moved, since they
would be orphaned;
.Cm edit
moves it.
.It Fl T , Fl -todo
.Cm list
and
.Cm add :
tasks
.Pq Ql VTODO ,
not events.
.It Fl t Ar when , Fl -to Ns = Ns Ar when
The end of the window, 30 days after its start by default.
.It Fl U Ar url , Fl -url Ns = Ns Ar url
The calendar, by its URL
.Pq Sx The server and the calendar .
.It Fl u Ar user , Fl -user Ns = Ns Ar user
The user
.Pq Sx Credentials .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width CALDAV_PASSWORD
.It Ev CALDAV_PASSWORD
The password, when
.Fl p
is not given.
.It Ev CALDAV_USER
The user, when
.Fl u
is not given.
.It Ev EDITOR
The editor of
.Cm edit ;
.Xr vi 1
by default.
.It Ev EMAIL
The user, when nothing else names one
.Pq Sx Credentials ;
the address that git and mail tools read too.
.It Ev TZ
The local time zone, in which times are read and written.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.authinfo , Pa ~/.netrc
The user and password, as the entry of the server
.Pq Ql machine host login user password secret .
.It Pa ~/.local/share/caldav/
Where
.Fl B
saves events by default.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
A failure: the server or the network failed or refused, no event or
several matched, the event changed on the server meanwhile, some
objects of an
.Cm import
were refused, or there is no user or password.
.It 2
A usage error: an unknown option or command, a missing argument, a
.Ar when ,
.Ar length ,
regex or URL that does not parse, or an end that is not after the
start.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
This week and the next, in every calendar:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ caldav -A -t +14 list
.Ed
.Pp
A dentist's appointment, with a reminder the day before:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ caldav add -S Dentist --start 'tomorrow 9:30' --duration 45 \e
    -l 'Rue du Port' --alarm 1d
.Ed
.Pp
Move it an hour later, keeping the old version:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ caldav -B set Dentist --start 'tomorrow 10:30'
.Ed
.Pp
A task, then the tasks still open, as JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ caldav -T add -S 'Renew the passport' --due 2026-11-30
$ caldav -T -j list
.Ed
.Pp
Save the work calendar, then restore it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ caldav -c Work backup work.ics
$ caldav -c Work import work.ics
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr carddav 1
.Sh STANDARDS
.Bl -tag -width "RFC 6764" -compact
.It RFC 4791
Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV).
.It RFC 5545
Internet Calendaring and Scheduling Core Object Specification
(iCalendar).
.It RFC 6764
Locating Services for Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV) and
vCard Extensions to WebDAV (CardDAV); the SRV and TXT records.
.El
.Sh CAVEATS
Storing an event one organises, with attendees, makes a server that
schedules implicitly, as Cyrus does, mail them the invitation or the
update:
.Cm add ,
.Cm set ,
.Cm edit
and
.Cm import
all do so.
.Pp
.Fl p
puts the password on the command line, where
.Xr ps 1
shows it to every user, and
.Fl -debug
prints it, in the Authorization header; the entry in
.Pa ~/.authinfo
shows it to nobody.
.Pp
.Nm
is Ruby, unlike the other tools of systools: it needs Ruby 3.4 or later
and the nokogiri gem.
